Track 2: Ain't You Missing the Four-Fifteen?
- Story & Context: Holed up in an abandoned weigh station off Highway 11W, Maeve Cornett recorded this vocal confession directly into an SP-404 powered by half-dead batteries while smelling lukewarm chicory coffee and damp pine sawdust. She had missed her sister's wedding down in Cocke County because the crew spent thirty-six hours rewiring highway junction boxes to slow down gravel-truck convoys. The song’s lazy, stuttering groove came from a contact mic taped to an oscillating transit flasher relay that clicked like an unsteady pocketwatch against the concrete floor. "I left my Tuesday sitting on your porch," she draws out over the gut-string bass thuds, capturing the private grief of sacrificing family time for a highway war nobody else understood.
